<onlyinclude>A '''neural processing unit''' (NPU) is a chip or part of a chip that speeds up certain calculations used by artificial neural networks. It can help a device run tasks such as recognizing images or processing speech without relying on a general-purpose processor for every calculation.</onlyinclude>

'''Reading depth:''' [[Neural processing unit/Simple|Simple]] · [[Neural processing unit/Technical|Technical]]

== How it works ==
A neural network repeatedly combines numbers called ''weights'' with input numbers called ''activations''. Many NPUs have hardware that performs large numbers of multiplications and additions in parallel. They also try to reuse data stored close to the computing units, because moving data from distant memory can take time and energy.

== Where it is used ==
An NPU can form part of a phone, small embedded device, or larger computer system. For example, Arm's Ethos-U family targets machine-learning acceleration in embedded systems. Other designs are built for different power budgets and workloads.

== What it is not ==
An NPU is not a replacement for every CPU or GPU task. It may support only certain neural-network layers, data formats, or model sizes. Software can send unsupported operations to the CPU or another processor. An NPU does not guarantee that a model is accurate, private, fast, or energy-efficient in every application.

